  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 126 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nEarly into the middle of next week, split upper-level flow will \ncontinue across North America as an amplified ridge remains \nestablished across the northern Plains. This pattern will favor the \npolar jet stream remaining displaced north of the forecast area, \nwhile the southern-stream jet remains farther south across the \nsouthern CONUS. With the strongest forcing for ascent remaining \ndisplaced north and south of the region, generally dry conditions \nare expected across western and north-central Nebraska. Through \nWednesday, the NBM and GEFS/EPS ensemble guidance continue to favor \nabove-normal temperatures, with highs generally reaching the lower \nto middle 60s across much of the area. Persistent upper-level \nridging, along with anomalously warm temperatures in the lower to \nmiddle levels, should support continued above-normal temperatures \nand limited precipitation chances through the middle of next week. \nBy Thursday, forecast uncertainty begins to increase as differences \nemerge in the evolution of the upper-level pattern. The GFS/GEFS \nfavors a trough digging farther south and west beneath the ridge \nacross southern Canada, while the ECMWF/EPS maintains this feature \nfarther east. Should the trough develop farther west and progress \nsouthward, a return to cooler temperatures would become more likely \nacross western and north-central Nebraska. However, if the trough \nremains farther east, upper-level ridging may persist across the \nregion, allowing above-normal temperatures to continue. Confidence \nremains low regarding the eventual evolution of this feature given \nthe differences among ensemble guidance and the extended forecast \nrange. Regardless, precipitation chances are expected to remain low \nthrough much of the long-term period, with the primary storm tracks \ngenerally remaining north and south of the forecast area.",
